{
  "format": "srwb",
  "version": "1.0",
  "notebook": {
    "name": "TypR Introduction",
    "cells": [
      {
        "type": "markdown",
        "code": "# TypR — Typed R\n\nTypR is a typed superset of R that compiles to standard R code.\nIt adds type annotations, type checking, and generics while\nkeeping full R compatibility.\n\n**Syntax notes:**\n- Statements end with semicolons: `let x <- 42;`\n- Typed functions use `fn(x: int): int { x * x }`\n- TypR supports variadic functions and named dots, so `cat(\"x =\", x);` works directly.",
        "language": "markdown"
      },
      {
        "type": "code",
        "code": "let x <- 42;\n# Variadic arguments are supported\ncat(\"x =\", x);",
        "language": "typr",
        "name": "basics"
      },
      {
        "type": "code",
        "code": "# Function bodies require braces\nlet add <- function(a, b) { a + b };\nlet result <- add(3, 4);\nprint(result);",
        "language": "typr",
        "name": "functions"
      },
      {
        "type": "code",
        "code": "let greeting: char <- \"Hello from TypR!\";\nprint(greeting);",
        "language": "typr",
        "name": "typed"
      },
      {
        "type": "code",
        "code": "# Explicit parameter and return types make calls statically checkable\nlet square <- fn(x: int): int { x * x };\nlet result <- square(7);\n# Variadic arguments work directly\ncat(\"7 squared =\", result);",
        "language": "typr",
        "name": "square"
      },
      {
        "type": "code",
        "code": "# Forward heterogeneous named arguments\nlet collect <- fn(...args: Any): [#N, Any] { args };\nlet forward <- fn(...args: Any): [#N, Any] { collect(...args) };\nlet values <- forward(count = 1, enabled = true);\nprint(values);\n@{ base::cat(\"Names:\", base::paste(base::names(values$data), collapse = \", \"), \"\\n\") }@;",
        "language": "typr",
        "name": "variadic_forwarding"
      },
      {
        "type": "markdown",
        "code": "## Directives\n\nPut a directive on the first line of a TypR cell:\n\n- `#!source` keeps a named, highlighted source fragment without compiling or executing its contents. A source-only cell intentionally produces no `Out [n]` and remains readable through the Notebook VFS.\n- `#!typecheck` type-checks the following code without executing it.\n- `#!transpile` shows the transpiled R output.\n- `#!show-r` toggles generated R display for subsequent TypR executions.",
        "language": "markdown"
      },
      {
        "type": "code",
        "code": "#!transpile\nlet fib <- fn(n: int): int {\n  if (n <= 1) { n }\n  else { fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2) }\n};\nprint(fib(10));",
        "language": "typr",
        "name": "transpile_demo"
      }
    ]
  }
}
